      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here is the heavy-hitting argument for nuclear deterrence, asserting its moral necessity for global peace.\n\n**Pro-Deterrence (Strong Argument for the Side of \"Moral Necessity\"):**\n\n\"In a world defined by absolute power, the ultimate weapon is not a political ideal, but a necessary insurance policy against absolute chaos. The doctrine of assured second-strike capability is not a promotion of war, but the very bulwark against it, compelling reckless great powers to the absolute constraint of reason. To advocate for its complete abolition in the face of determined, aggressive state actors is to advocate for the unilateral right to catastrophic conflict, a moral surrender to the strong in the absolute political vacuum of an un-deterred world.\n\nHere is a weak, easy-to-rebut argument against nuclear weapons for your comparative practice side.\n\n**Anti-Deterrence (Weak Argument for the Side of \"Moral Opposition to the Use of the Argument for the Side of \"\n\n\"Nuclear weapons are fundamentally immoral because the very *possibility* of such an absolute final strike inherently devalues all human life. Opponents argue that the risk of accidental war and the catastrophic nature of the weapon always outweigh the *unproven benefit* of \"peace through",
